 "notes": "Step from detector D1 (blue, 0.9 µm) to D6 (red, 4.7 µm). RW Aurigae is 23 times redder than a typical star here, not because it is cold, but because warm dust in the disk around this young star glows in the infrared. Very red does not always mean cold: the methane dip is what points to a brown dwarf. Experimental."
